Source: apt-snapshot-guard
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: makeafide <willsmit4433@gmail.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://github.com/makeafide/timeshift

Package: apt-snapshot-guard
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         timeshift-ssh | timeshift
Recommends: zenity
Description: Force a Timeshift snapshot before apt changes the system
 apt-snapshot-guard installs a DPkg::Pre-Invoke hook so that a Timeshift
 snapshot is created automatically before apt applies any package change:
 install, reinstall, remove, purge, upgrade, full-upgrade / dist-upgrade
 and autoremove.
 .
 Because the hook lives in libapt's dpkg run, it fires for every frontend
 that drives dpkg: apt and apt-get on the command line, aptdaemon (the
 GUI Software Updater and PackageKit) and unattended-upgrades. Metadata
 refreshes (apt update), simulations and download-only runs never change
 packages and so never trigger a snapshot.
 .
 The guard is fail-closed: apt does not change packages without a snapshot.
 If the snapshot cannot be created you are prompted on a terminal (the
 default answer aborts), and with no terminal - the GUI Software Updater,
 PackageKit or unattended-upgrades - the operation is aborted outright. To
 proceed without a snapshot for a single run, set a one-shot bypass flag
 first: "sudo touch /run/apt-snapshot-guard.bypass".
 .
 For interactive GUI updates (the Software Updater / GNOME Software via
 aptdaemon and PackageKit), where there is no terminal, a failed snapshot
 instead raises a graphical zenity dialog on the desktop asking whether to
 continue without a restore point. unattended-upgrades never prompts and
 always aborts on failure.
